	abstract = {The combination of Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) with Federated Learning (FL) enables intelligent analytics on low-power microcontrollers. While prior often rely on resource-rich devices (e.g., Raspberry Pi, Jetson), very few have demonstrated FL on low-power devices. This research develops a resource-limited federated learning system which operates on ESP32 devices to perform real-time health monitoring. A lightweight neural network processed parameters like blood oxygen saturation (SpO<sub>2</sub>), heart rate and temperature. The devices run local anomaly detection while sending model updates to build a global model through federated averaging. Unlike centralized TinyML deployments, our framework maintains privacy by keeping all raw health data on the device. The proposed system reaches an average classification accuracy of 98 % while maintaining inference latency under 100 ms and keeping the quantized model size below 3 KB to support deployment on memory-restricted hardware. These results demonstrate that federated aggregation preserves accuracy while reducing centralized storage needs enabling privacy-preserving and resourceefficient solution. © 2025 IEEE.},
