	abstract = {Indoor crop production requires multiple trials to determine which conditions and parameters help in optimizing plant growth. A solution for this problem is to promptly detect nutrient deficiency. This paper proposes an artificial intelligence of things (AIoT) -based system that aims to monitor plant growth and detect stunted plant growth caused by nutrient deficiency. The proposed system comprises of wireless sensor nodes that periodically capture growth box images and record relevant environmental data. The system was installed in an experimental greenhouse in De La Salle Araneta University Salikneta Farm. Lettuce crops were cultivated using Kratky hydroponics, a soilless cultivation method popularly used in urban areas. To test the system's capability of detecting nutrient deficiencies, varying amounts of a liquid fish-based nutrient solution called fermented fish amino acid (FFAA) were applied to different treatment and control groups. The growth box images were collected and used to train a deep learning-based model for segmenting plant images and measuring the leaf area of each plant. The proposed algorithm generated an average Jaccard index as high as 0.82 relative to manual annotations. The leaf area of the plants was measured manually and was significantly similar to the automatically collected growth level values (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.94), as proven by Mann-Whitney U-test. Finally, anomaly detection methods were employed to detect plant nutrient deficiencies in each growth box. The proposed system can be a practical tool for farm managers to reduce risks and economic loss in indoor crop production. © 2022 ASABE. All Rights Reserved.},
