	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 International Conference on Communication and Smart Devices, ICCoSD 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCOSD66074.2025.11348379},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041763748?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning, Dayananda Sagar College of Engineering, Karnataka, Bangalore, India; Department of Computer Science, College of Computer Science, King Khalid University, Aseer, Abha, Saudi Arabia; Department of Science and Humanities, Vishwakarma Institute of Technology Haveli, Maharashtra, India; Department of ECE, Bharati Vidyapeeth College of Engineering, New Delhi, 110063, India; Lincoln University, Malaysia; Department of English, Kongu Engineering College, Perundurai, Tamilnadu, Erode, India; Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, St.Joseph's Institute of Technology, OMR, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {Wearable IoT devices: With the fast development of wearable technologies in the field of IoT, there is now the possibility of constant monitoring of the health of individuals and spotting any anomaly at an early stage, which can be greatly beneficial in providing personal health care. Nevertheless, it is still not hard to imagine using machine learning (ML) in resource-constrained edge devices because of the shortages in memory, energy, and processing capabilities. The current paper explores the most recent breakthroughs in the area of low-power ML models - namely, TinyML, model pruning, and quantization - that enables real-time anomaly detection in wearable healthcare systems. Besides the overview of edge computing frameworks (TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers and Edge Impulse), the given research provides a mathematical model and parametric investigation of a TinyML-based anomaly detection model. Through emulating a shallow CNN on the ARM Cortex-M4 architecture in respect to detecting ECG anomalies, we evaluate how well different models perform along with different latency, energy, and accuracy limits. Findings demonstrate that model size can be minimized by ~83%, energy use by ~65%, as well as latency by~61.” Compared to current compressed deep learning models, our results match them and are even better, which demonstrates the feasibility of shallow TinyML models in realistic edge-based healthcare systems that have low latencies, low power, and high accuracy. In this paper, the concluding observations are made on the scalability, interpretability, and moral concerns, which lead to a systematic roadmap on developing an effective wearable health system that is not only secure but also intelligent. © 2025 IEEE.},
