	abstract = {This paper presents a TinyML system for end-to-end, on-device vibration-based mechanical fault detection in single-phase induction motors. The sensing node integrates a low-cost MEMS inertial sensor (LSM6DSO) with an ESP32-C3 microcontroller. The proposed firmware implements orientation-invariant preprocessing, contiguous leakage-safe segmentation, and int8 quantized inference of a compact multi-channel 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Window-level outputs are aggregated using a confidence-weighted majority voting rule to provide robust diagnoses across four states: motorOFF, motorON, motorON_NoFan, and motorON_BadFan. By employing strictly contiguous file splits to prevent temporal data leakage, we report a window-level macro F1-score of 0.98 and a recording-level macro F1-score of 0.95 on held-out, position-specific data. The quantized model requires only 80 KB of Flash and 120 KB of RAM, with an inference latency of 82 ms. This demonstrates the feasibility of deploying sophisticated diagnostic AI on low-power edge devices for real-time industrial condition monitoring of induction motors. © 2026 IEEE.},
