	affiliations = {Department of Computer Science, Multidisciplinary Faculty of Nador, University Mohammed Premier, Oujda, Morocco; Laboratory LaSTI, Department of Networks, Telecommunications and Cybersecurity, ENSA Khouribga, Sultan Moulay Slimane University, Beni Mellal, Morocco},
	abstract = {TinyML, the deployment of machine learning models on resource-constrained edge devices, is emerging as a transformative technology with applications spanning smart vehicles, anomaly detection, smart farming, healthcare, and beyond. This chapter provides a comprehensive exploration of TinyML, detailing its development workflow, challenges, and innovative solutions. By enabling real-time, low-latency, and energy-efficient inference at the edge, TinyML addresses critical limitations of cloud-centric approaches, such as connectivity dependency and privacy concerns. This chapter outlines the TinyML development process, from system design and model training to deployment and continuous monitoring, while highlighting key challenges like hardware heterogeneity, model optimization, and the lack of standardized benchmarks. Proposed solutions, including hardware-aware compilers, AutoML tools, and event-driven processing, are discussed to overcome these obstacles. Additionally, this chapter showcases diverse applications, such as autonomous vehicles, predictive maintenance in industrial systems, and precision agriculture, demonstrating TinyML’s potential to revolutionize industries and improve quality of life. Future research directions emphasize the need for interdisciplinary collaboration, agile methodologies, and standardized evaluation frameworks to accelerate TinyML adoption. As the field evolves, TinyML is poised to play a pivotal role in shaping the future of edge intelligence, offering scalable and sustainable solutions for global challenges. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Khalid El Makkaoui, Ismail Lamaakal, Ibrahim Ouahbi, Yassine Maleh, and Ahmed A. Abd El-Latif; individual chapters, the contributors.},
