	affiliations = {School of Computing, Newcastle University, Newcastle upon Tyne, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Lifelong anomaly detection holds immense promise for enabling on-device intelligence in the Internet of Things (IoT), as devices need to adapt to evolving environments continuously. State-of-the-art algorithms based on random sub-sampling and the Local Outlier Factor (LOF) have demonstrated high accuracy in experiments. However, their reliance on Python and resourceintensive scientific computing libraries has created a critical deployment gap, preventing their use on real-world, resourceconstrained microcontrollers (MCUs). This paper bridges this gap by presenting STREAM-LAD, the first high-performance, self-contained C implementation of this lifelong learning framework, with a seamless Python wrapper to facilitate rapid prototyping and evaluation. We demonstrate that our implementation achieves full accuracy parity. More importantly, we show that our C-accelerated version provides a modest but consistent latency reduction while achieving an order-of-magnitude reduction in deployment footprint (binary size and memory), enabling its deployment on a real MCU-based hardware. By providing the first open-source, stream-native implementation of this framework, we provide a foundational tool for the next wave of research into practical, lifelong learning on continuous data streams. © 2025 IEEE.},
