	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035765802?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {University of Bremen, Bremen, Germany},
	abstract = {Resilience is a critical requirement for dependable IoT systems, enabling them to maintain reliable operation despite faults. One common source of such faults is inaccurate sensor data, which may result from hardware malfunctions or noise. While advances in fault detection have improved anomaly identification in sensor readings, detection alone is insufficient. Truly resilient systems must also incorporate recovery mechanisms to mitigate the impact of faults. Simply flagging or discarding abnormal values can disrupt data continuity and degrade the performance of downstream applications. To address this challenge, we propose a resource-efficient fault correction framework for real-time operation in multi-sensor IoT systems. Our approach leverages temporal and spatial data patterns by extracting informative features from recent readings of the affected sensor and its correlated peers. This joint representation captures short-term dynamics and contextual cues, enabling the model to bridge external information from correlated sensors into the temporal dynamics of the target sensor. These enriched features are then processed by a lightweight LSTM-based model to reconstruct readings with minimal computational overhead. We validate the effectiveness of our approach through experiments on real-world sensor datasets, demonstrating low reconstruction error and support for edge deployment via a TinyML implementation. Furthermore, experimental results show that the framework can successfully correct faulty sensor readings even when tested on previously unseen data characterized by patterns and distributions distinct from the training data, highlighting its robustness and generalization capability. This sustained correction capability complements existing fault detection methods, forming a unified and resilient solution for dependable IoT operation.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Fault correction; Internet of Things (IoT); resilience; sensor data quality; TinyML},
