	affiliations = {Viettel Group, Hanoi, Viet Nam},
	abstract = {We present a non-invasive approach to monitor the conditions of BLDC fuel pumps using an external microphone. A MEMS microphone (SPH0645LM4H-B) is placed outside the pump casing at a fixed distance and angle-without any intervention to the fuel line-and digitized by an STM32F407VET6 acquisition platform. Data were collected at 1000rpm-2atm, 2000rpm-5atm, and 3000rpm-5atm, each under two operating states: Normal and Aeration. The processing pipeline comprises Welch power spectral density (PSD), high-resolution short-time Fourier transform (STFT) with explicit k × BPF line overlays, and Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCC). Run-level features emphasize the tonal-broadband decomposition, including BPFP (energy ratio around BPF±40Hz to the broadband 0.5-8kHz), <sub>BPF</sub> ± 40Hz(dB) harmonic prominence, spectral tonalness, and HF/LF ratios. Statistical analysis (Welch's t-test, 95% confidence intervals, and Hedges' g) reveals significant differences between Normal and Aeration (p<0.05), while pressure variation induces systematic shifts in spectral energy distribution. The results demonstrate that a non-invasive microphone is a cost-effective solution for early anomaly detection and load estimation, and is suitable for real-time deployment (TinyML) on embedded hardware.  © 2025 IEEE.},
