	affiliations = {Loyola-ICAM College of Engineering and Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {In this work, we propose a new edge computing framework for fault detection of industrial machines based on sound vibration analysis. A low footprint TinyML model has been used and deployed on the Arduino Nano RP2040 microcontroller via TensorFlow Lite. The machines' acoustic signals are evaluated for unusual patterns indicative of damage or wear. Features are extracted by the MelFrequency Energy (MFE), which is introduced to effectively represent spectral properties of the audio signal related to the machine condition monitoring. Conventional fault detection systems are generally based on cloud services, which inevitably rely on continuous data to be transmitted from the edge devices to the remote servers for the purpose of processing and inferencing. This solution comes with the added latency, power usage and network dependency and security risks. In contrast, the proposed edge computing approach enhances extensive work on scene graph generation and detection. online inference on the embedded device. This provides immediate fault finding, low latency, higher data security, and removes the requirement of always-on cloud connection. Local processing of data reduces Operative Costs due to less bandwidth consumption and less dependence on Cloud services. It may save energy, thus making it suitable for use in the industrial area which is far away from the city or lack of energy sources. By reducing the response time any potential for delays, which can often manifest as system failures or downtime, is kept at a minimal. The paper also provides empirical evaluation and comparative studies to show that the edge-based model is more effective than the conventional cloud approach. In summary, this work showcases the promise of TinyML and edge computing for smart, cost-effective, and energy-efficient fault detection in industrial applications.  © 2025 IEEE.},
