	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034777398?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {City University of Hong Kong, Department of Electrical Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong; City University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen Research Institute, Shenzhen, 518057, China},
	abstract = {Reliable power line monitoring is critical for consumer energy systems that increasingly depend on distributed sensors, smart terminals, and IoT-enabled edge devices. In practical deployments, fault detection performance is often affected by changing operating conditions, noisy measurements, and unreliable prediction confidence. This paper proposes a risk-aware AIoT-based fault detection framework that integrates multi-domain signal representation, uncertainty-aware inference, and confidence-adaptive decision making. High-frequency waveform signals are analyzed across time, frequency, and wavelet domains to capture complementary fault characteristics, and are adaptively fused to preserve localized and nonstationary patterns. Instead of producing deterministic outputs, a Dirichlet-based Prior Network is employed to jointly estimate fault probability and predictive uncertainty. These quantities are combined into a scalar risk score that enables a three-outcome decision strategy, allowing the system to issue alarms, defer uncertain cases, or identify normal operation. Experiments on a real-world power line dataset show that the proposed approach maintains stable detection performance and well-calibrated confidence under distribution shift. The results demonstrate that incorporating uncertainty-aware inference and risk-adaptive decision logic improves the reliability of AI-enabled monitoring systems, making the framework suitable for IoT-driven consumer energy applications.  © 1975-2011 IEEE.},
