	abstract = {Indoor air quality (IAQ) critically affects occupant health, comfort, and energy efficiency in smart homes. Most current IAQ solutions focus on pollutant detection and visualization, offering limited support for adaptive, user-specific appliance control. This paper presents AirWise, a large language model (LLM)-based framework that integrates real-Time indoor and outdoor environmental data with user profiles, including health conditions, comfort preferences, and appliance availability, to provide personalized, context-Aware control recommendations. AirWise leverages the Environmental Monitoring Device (EMD) on the iAirGuard platform, which ensures high-quality input by denoising sensor data and using TinyML-based fault detection. The LLM functions as a high-level decision engine, dynamically adjusting AC mode, fan speed, and window/door states, balancing pollutant mitigation, comfort, and energy efficiency. The framework was evaluated in a real smart home testbed under severe pollution conditions. During experiments with an AC equipped with a filter, indoor PM2.5 and PM10 AQI dropped from 6 (Severe) to 2 (Satisfactory) within 50 minutes. LLM inference consumed less than 5% of system tokens, demonstrating real-Time applicability. AirWise offers a scalable, adaptive, and energy-Aware solution for personalized IAQ management. © 2026 IEEE.},
