	abstract = {This paper presents a fabricated ultra-low-power system-on-chip that couples a RISC-V host with a dataflow-reconfigurable ML engine, embedded non-volatile MRAM for state retention, and a hierarchical power-domain scheme for duty-cycled sensing. The digital architecture integrates a SIMD array with precision-scalable MACs, on-chip L1/L2 SRAM, TCDM and APB interconnects, a uDMA for peripheral streaming, and an always-on controller that orchestrates clocking, isolation, and power gating for deep sleep and low-latency wake-up. Design features include runtime switching between MMM and MVM dataflows, block-structured sparsity with zero-skipping support, and hardware hooks for deconvolution and SVM norm evaluation to broaden workload coverage beyond CNNs. Silicon measurements on the 22-nm FD-SOI node validate energy-proportional operation across wide voltage-frequency ranges, demonstrating sub-milliwatt always-on inference and micro-watt deep-sleep retention suitable for extreme-edge deployments. End-to-end tests with audio and anomaly-detection pipelines confirm that co-designed hardware and software stacks can sustain real-time throughput while meeting stringent PPAC targets in embedded electrical systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
