	affiliations = {Lanzhou Vocational Technical College, Lanzhou, China},
	abstract = {Agricultural data collected in AIoT-enabled greenhouses are often corrupted by noise, leading to outliers and missing values that degrade analysis reliability. To address this, we propose a time series reconstruction method based on a Radial Basis Function (RBF) neural network, which exploits the nonlinear and temporal characteristics of environmental sensor data. The pipeline includes data cleaning, stationarity testing, normalization, and RBF model training using K-means clustering for center selection and least squares for weight optimization. Evaluated on real temperature data from farms in Gansu, China, the method is compared against the Autoregressive Moving Average (ARMA) model-specifically the ARMA(2,10) configuration, which has been widely adopted as a benchmark in agricultural time series analysis due to its demonstrated superiority over simple imputation techniques such as mean or nearest-neighbor filling. Experimental results show that the proposed RBF-based approach significantly outperforms ARMA(2,10) in both anomaly correction and missing value imputation, achieving substantially lower Average Relative Error (ARE) and Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). The method demonstrates consistent accuracy in reconstructing randomly removed data points and effectively handles the nonstationary, nonlinear nature of agricultural time series, thereby enhancing data quality and supporting more reliable decision-making in smart farming applications. © 2026 The Authors.},
