	affiliations = {University of Calabria, Modeling, Electronics and Systems, Department of Computer Engineering, Rende, Italy},
	abstract = {Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) remain the leading cause of mortality globally. Continuous, non-invasive monitoring presents a critical opportunity for the early detection of Adverse Cardiac Events (ACEs). This paper proposes a hierarchical edge-cloud architecture that leverages the ubiquity of wearable sensors, the analytical power of Deep Learning (DL), and the efficiency of Edge Computing for a proactive cardiac alert system. We design a two-tier framework: (1) A lightweight, 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) optimized via TinyML principles is deployed on an edge device (e.g., a smartphone) for real-time analysis of Electrocardiogram (ECG) signals and initial anomaly detection. (2) Upon detection of a high-risk pattern, relevant ECG segments are offloaded to a cloud-based server where a more complex Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) or Transformer-based model performs deep contextual analysis to confirm the threat and assess the risk level. This hybrid approach optimizes battery life, reduces response latency, and enhances patient privacy by minimizing raw data transmission. Experimental validation on the public MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database demonstrates high classification accuracy for critical arrhythmias and showcases the system's potential to revolutionize proactive cardiac telemedicine. © 2025 IEEE.},
