	abstract = {Continuous health monitoring in resourcelimited healthcare settings remains a persistent challenge, primarily due to the disparity between high-cost, real-time surveillance systems and the inadequacies of episodic care. Unlike previous IoT-based monitoring solutions that often operate in isolation, this research presents an integrated approach, employing affordable ESP32 hardware in tandem with a hybrid stacking ensemble model and multi-role clinical dashboards to ensure real-time applicability. Specifically, the proposed AIoT framework brings together ESP32-based multisensor platforms and a novel stacking ensemble that fuses Random Forest and Artificial Neural Network classifiers, achieving a notable 95.45 % accuracy rate in detecting eight distinct health anomalies-surpassing the typical 88-95% benchmark of existing systems. The framework's performance is validated through synthetic physiological data, demonstrating effective real-time anomaly detection while maintaining computational efficiency on resource-constrained devices. This technology is promising for applications such as rural clinics, home-elderly care, and post-operation monitoring, in which there is no access to conventional infrastructure. However, the study is limited by its reliance on synthetic datasets and the necessity for subsequent clinical trials and regulatory clearance before real-world implementation. Overall, the framework substantiates the practical viability of affordable, continuous monitoring within resource-limited clinical environments, while facilitating collaborative healthcare delivery through role-based access controls.  © 2025 IEEE.},
