	abstract = {The paper iAirGuard is introduced, the open-source, modular, and wireless sensor network intended for large-scale and affordable air quality scrutiny. Conventional high-precision stations, which are scattered, still set a high price and are not widely maintained, while many low-cost systems encounter the problems of drifting calibration, low reliability, and reliance on centralization. iAirGuard's limitation is solved with a service centric architecture structured in four layers where the layers are; sensor nodes, data storage, service modules, and user interfaces. Communication between the nodes, which are based on ESP32-S3 and have PM, CO, CO2 and VOC sensors with BLE/Wi-Fi connectivity, is done through a lightweight MQTT protocol. The framework puts forward three main innovations: (i) adaptive dynamic sampling which modifies the sampling rates based on real-time environmental variations to save power and prolong the life of the sensor; (ii) on-node TinyML-based AQI prediction for real-time inference; and (iii) fault-resilient secondary sensing that uses threshold-based learning mechanisms to identify sensor drift. The experimental tests kitchen, office and outdoors reveal that the reduction of storage and bandwidth is between 45 and 58 percent, consequently, the daily data transmission is down from 0.275 MB to 0.155 MB while still giving 100 percent synchronization reliability. The entire hardware, firmware, and software stack being open-source allows transparency, reproducibility, and extensibility, thus, iAirGuard is characterized as a strong, cheap, and ecological system for IoT-enabled air quality monitoring over the long term. © 2026 IEEE.},
