	affiliations = {Vivekanandha College of Arts and Sciences for Women (Autonomous), Department of Computer Science and Applications, Tamilnadu, India},
	abstract = {Areas susceptible to natural disasters experience recurring interruptions in power supply, wherein conventional centralized cloud designs falter with network latency, connectivity reliance, and excessive power consumption. To overcome such constraints, the present work introduces a greener green edge-cloud collaborative architecture that combines TinyML- enabled edge devices with a cloud platform supported by renewable energy sources. The edge layer provides real-time local anomaly detection and monitoring, guaranteeing autonomous operation in the event of connectivity outage, while the cloud utilizes LSTM models for short-term and long-term demand forecasting. To promote trust and transparency, SHAP-based explainability is introduced, and a voice-based emergency alert module provides multilingual speech notifications, rendering the system accessible to both technical and non-technical users, as well as visually impaired people. Experimental testing through the iFogSim simulator proves to be much improved: 30 % less energy consumption, 25 % improved prediction accuracy, and below-3-second alert latency versus traditional solutions. This indicates that the system is capable of running efficiently in both urban and rural environments. In the future, the platform can be augmented with blockchain-enabled energy trading, more renewable sources, and adaptive reinforcement learning models to further enhance resilience, scalability, and sustainability of microgrid ecosystems. © 2026 IEEE.},
