	abstract = {This paper presents a machine condition-based maintenance approach that leverages vibration data and embedded deep learning for real-time machine fault detection. Vibration signals were collected by a machine running at 1720 rpm and processed at a sampling rate of 1 Hz. Spectral features such as spectral power and RMS were extracted using FFT and then used to train a deep neural network classifier model. The model achieved a high accuracy of 96.9% in classifying machine faults, including faulty bearings, faulty belts, combined bearing and belt faults, and normal and failure conditions. The model has been optimized for use on a microcontroller. The model's performance on a device with TensorFlow Lite and the Edge Optimized Neural Compiler is evaluated. The results show that the classifier model is suitable for real-time condition monitoring using an ESP32 processor. The results show that accurate machine fault classification can be achieved even with a lower sampling rate, making this solution feasible for edge computing applications in industrial environments. ©2025 IEEE.},
