	affiliations = {Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Sri Venkateswara College of Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Sriperumbudur, India; Department of Computing Technologies, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Tamil Nadu, Kattankulathur, India; Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology, Tamil Nadu, Sriperumbudur, India},
	abstract = {There is an increasing need for women’s safety and security in many environments which necessitates innovative and technological solutions. For this, we propose a smart wearable that performs wrist authentication, health monitoring, location data analysis, and an SOS alerting system. The smart wearable will be a bracelet-like structure that comprises various sensors to monitor and authenticate, and TinyML for machine learning to detect anomalies and generate SOS alerts. The wearable will contain unique wrist biometric data of the user, which cannot be altered. If someone else tries to wear it, it will alert the original user that “someone else is wearing it”. If there is no action for the alert after 5–10 minutes, then the alert will be sent to the server. From the server room, they will try to contact the original user, analyze the last few minutes of the wearable sensor’s status, and find the current location. If the location is prone to crimes, then reinforcements will be sent. The wearable will monitor SpO2 level, motion pattern with the help of accelerometer, and location data with the help of GPS. If there is abnormal behavior in the readings, an alert will be sent to the server room that there is an anomaly in behavior. By using TinyML, the bracelet can perform complex data processing in a very efficient manner. It facilitates timely analysis and response. It reduces the need to contact the server and performs needed analysis at the local level. It not only reduces communication time but also reduces the consumption of resources such as memory and battery. Hence, it results in cost savings. This sleek design of wearable technology offers an effective solution ensuring women’s safety and security. The project aims to deliver a solution that is affordable to everyone. The estimated cost of each bracelet is around Rs.2000–4000, balancing quality and accuracy. Thus, it ensures proactive protection and decision-making that the victim is under control 