	affiliations = {Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences(SIMATS), Saveetha School of Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Thandalam, India},
	abstract = {Wearable Internet of Things (IoT) devices continuously generate multimodal sensor data for health and activity monitoring. However, cloud-based anomaly detection systems introduce latency, energy overhead, and significant privacy risks. This work proposes a privacy-aware Transformer-based TinyML framework for real-time multimodal anomaly detection directly on wearable devices. The architecture incorporates attention-based multimodal fusion, low-rank self-attention optimization, structured pruning, and 8-bit quantization for ultra-low-power microcontroller deployment. Differential Privacy Stochastic Gradient Descent (DP-SGD) is integrated during training to safeguard sensitive biometric information. Experimental evaluation on WESAD, PAMAP2, and HHAR datasets demonstrates superior accuracy (91.6 ± 0.4%), reduced latency (8.2 ms), and lower energy consumption (9.6 mJ) compared to CNN and LSTM baselines. Statistical testing confirms significant performance improvements (p < 0.01). The framework establishes a unified solution balancing accuracy, computational feasibility, and privacy preservation, enabling practical on-device intelligence for wearable IoT systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
