	affiliations = {National Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Durgapur, India; Dr. B. C. Roy Engineering College, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Durgapur, India; University of Alabama in Huntsville, Department of Mathematical Sciences, AL, United States},
	abstract = {Air pollution is a significant contributor to respiratory diseases, posing severe health risks globally. Traditional air quality monitoring stations, while effective, are too expensive, particularly in developing nations like India, where numerous monitoring sites may be required to cover diverse urban and industrial regions. Existing cost-effective air quality monitoring systems are rendered inadequate by their reliance on sensors that are prone to drift, which necessitates frequent recalibration. This study addresses the gap by developing a cost-effective air quality monitoring device capable of detecting particulate matter, carbon monoxide (CO), temperature, and humidity, and incorporating self-diagnostic capabilities. It enhances reliability through a dual-sensing mechanism: it calculates the Air Quality Index (AQI) directly and uses a TinyML model to provide secondary-sensed AQI estimations. Deviations between these two AQI values signal sensor malfunction, which is tracked by a proposed algorithm, Interocept, that assigns fault scores to the sensors. Models were created for eight cities, showing accuracies from 0.69-0.89 for dust and 0.76-0.95 for CO. These models were optimized for real-time performance by converting them into TensorFlow Lite micro-models, allowing deployment on the device itself for continuous, autonomous sensor health diagnostics and improved long-term air quality management systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
