	affiliations = {Princess Sumaya University for Technology, Jordan; Jordan University of Science and Technology (JUST), Jordan},
	abstract = {The combination of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Internet of Things (IoT) is referred to as the Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT). AIoT has transformed, modernized, and improved healthcare systems by optimizing healthcare operations, increasing the chance of decent patient outcomes, and creating unique solutions and opportunities. AIoT makes it easier to perform remote patient monitoring, predictive health diagnostics, and automated surgeries by linking the analytical power of AI to the ability of IoT in capturing real-time data. However, the application of AIoT remains to have significant drawbacks in the healthcare sector because of its security risks, such as vulnerabilities in medical devices, loss of data, and attacks targeting AI models or networks connecting IoT devices. Although AIoT in healthcare is a promising technology, it can cause severe cybersecurity issues related to the networked nature of IoT devices, patient information, and AI complexity. Some related case studies include the WannaCry ransomware targeting the UK’s National Health Service as well as flaws in the security features of pacemakers and insulin pumps, exposing the dangers of security breaches to people’s lives. It is evident from these incidences that AI technology should be developed in a manner that relies on encryption, compliance with advanced AI solution-based anomaly detection, and suitable communication protocols to protect patient information. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Mariyam Ouaissa, Mariya Ouaissa, Tarik Hidar, Ram Chandra Sachan, Akhil Mittal, and Sanjay Poddar; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.},
