	abstract = {Edge devices in 6G wireless networks are driving unprecedented levels of intelligence and autonomy, where billions of interconnected nodes collaboratively sense, learn, and adapt in real time. However, the coexistence of massive device heterogeneity, stringent resource constraints, and highly dynamic operational environments critically affects the stability and timeliness of decentralized model training and network management. The key challenge lies in achieving adaptive and reliable intelligence at the extreme edge, where computation, energy, and connectivity are fundamentally limited. To address this challenge, we propose a TinyML-driven self-healing framework that embeds lightweight learning agents into edge devices, enabling on-device analytics for local anomaly detection, adaptive self-recovery, and swarm-inspired collaboration for knowledge diffusion. Through model quantization and context-aware adaptation, TinyML effectively mitigates the impact of device heterogeneity by tailoring models to local capabilities, overcomes resource constraints via ultra-low-power inference, and sustains robustness under dynamic environments through distributed swarm coordination. A 6G-oriented smart factory case study demonstrates that, under these challenging conditions, our framework significantly improves fault detection latency, recovery speed, and communication efficiency compared with centralized solutions. Future work will focus on standardizing the TinyML-based self-healing architecture and defining interoperability interfaces to support large-scale deployment of resilient and autonomous Zero-Touch 6G Networks. © 2017 IEEE.},
