	affiliations = {National Louis University, United States; United States; College of Technical Engineering, The Islamic University, Department of Computers Techniques Engineering, Najaf, Iraq; Nitte Meenakshi Institute of Technology, Nitte (Deemed-to-be University), Department of (MBA), Bengaluru, India},
	abstract = {In recent years, the integration of blockchain and Internet of Things (IoT) technology has led to supply chains incorporating transparency to gain a competitive advantage. However, most systems explore blockchain-enabled architectures in a cloud atmosphere, which leads to latency and scalability problems. To address this, the proposed BlockchainEnabled Supply Chain Monitoring Framework using Edge Computing and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model optimized with TinyML Initially for collected IoT data, including radio frequency ID (RFID), Global Positioning System (GPS), and environmental sensors utilized to monitor the environment. First, preprocessed the edge nodes through normalization to ensure a clean and reliable input. Further, the LSTM model is instituted to capture the dependencies related to the time series data for accurate delay determination and anomaly detection, while the model is trained in a federated learning method, which separates the LSTM model from local people to enhance authentication. Optimization was achieved through structured weight pruning to remove unnecessary neurons and through post-training 8-bit quantization. The prediction and notifications were then recorded using the blockchain via a smart contract. Experimental results on the demonstrate that EC-LSTM-TinyML achieving higher accuracy (98.87%), precision (98.67%), recall (97.87%), and F1score (97.89%) when compared with existing model Gated Recurrent Unit- Multi-Head Attention (GRU-MHA). © 2025 IEEE.},
