	abstract = {The proposed work aims to minimize unplanned downtime and maintenance costs by implementing a vibrationbased predictive maintenance system for motor fault diagnosis. A Brushless DC (BLDC) motor integrated with a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B and an ADXL345 tri-Axial accelerometer is employed for real-Time vibration data acquisition at different operating speeds 1000, 1500, and 2000 RPM using an electronic speed controller. The acquired vibration signals are locally processed on the edge device, where a one-dimensional Convolutional Neural Network model classifies motor health conditions including normal operation, imbalance, misalignment, and bearing wear. Unlike conventional TinyML-based approaches that primarily focus on binary fault detection, the proposed system performs multi-class fault identification, enabling more precise condition monitoring. The diagnostic results are displayed on an OLED interface, facilitating rapid decisionmaking and real-Time monitoring. The edge-based implementation reduces latency, bandwidth dependency, and reliance on cloud infrastructure, making the system suitable for practical industrial predictive maintenance applications.  © 2026 IEEE.},
