	abstract = {Efficient Signal Processing (SP) enables quick pattern recognition from raw sensor data, reducing computational load by eliminating noise and emphasizing critical areas for accurate detection, enhancing system performance. The research introduces the Efficient Signal Extraction and Deep Fake-Focused Surveillance (ESEDFS) model for structural health monitoring and video authentication in limited-resource settings. It reduces system noise with a Butterworth filter (BWF) and utilizes data from 12 transducers to create 66 master baselines for analysis. Structural changes are quantified through feature metrics. For deep fake detection, CCTV frames are resized, normalized, and converted into tensors before input to an optimized EfficientNetB4-Lite model for real-time authentication. The ESEDFS framework is tailored for real-time on-device anomaly detection in edge environments. It focuses on nondestructive structural damage detection through ultrasonic- guided-waves and light deep fake detection for CCTV surveillance, operating without cloud computing or large-scale video forensics. Efficient microcontrollers incorporate features like dynamic range and quantization for effective model performance. The performance analysis is loss of (0.9, 0.6, 0.3), accuracy of 87% to 97%, ESEDFS overall of 92.1%, 91.2% accuracy and a 90.8% F1-score, ROC-AUC of 0.97. Calculation. © 2026 IEEE.},
