	abstract = {In energy systems, interoperability enables IoT infrastructures to share data and optimize operations, but integrating AI with communication technologies remains challenging, often reducing reliability. High retransmission rates and latency can delay anomaly detection and degrade real-time analysis. To address this, an intelligent energy monitoring system was deployed across selected sites in Cebu, capturing power reliability and IoT metrics. Data were securely transmitted via TLS to a Firebase database, and labeled datasets trained supervised and unsupervised machine learning models to detect anomalies in both power and network conditions. Network performance varied across sites, with latency from 1.0017 ms (San Fernando) to 1.7821 ms (Carmen), jitter from 0.16005 ms to 177.57 ms, retransmissions from 0.1697% to 43.887%, and throughput from 0.562 to 1.000. Sites such as San Fernando, Naga, and Punta had low latency and retransmissions under 1%, supporting stable control loops, while Carmen, Kodia, and SantaFe suffered high delays and jitter, compromising CPS responsiveness. The PSO-based performance score (0.108-2.090) integrated latency, jitter, retransmission, and throughput, and K-means clustering (k=2) identified two operational regimes (PSO = 0.263 vs. 1.723). Supervised models, including Decision Trees, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and MLPs, achieved perfect performance (precision, recall, F1 = 1.00), outperforming Logistic Regression (0.94), SVM (0.97), and Autoencoder (0.35). These results show that PSO-based metrics and machine learning can significantly enhance CPS interoperability in energy systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
