	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043836990?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Ningbo University, School of Artificial Intelligence, 315211, Taiwan; Tamkang University, Department of Computer Science and Information Engineering, New Taipei, 25137, Taiwan; National Taipei University of Business, Department of Information Management, Taipei, 100025, Taiwan; National Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Shillong, 793003, India},
	abstract = {Video anomaly detection (VAD) under weak supervision is pivotal for intelligent security and urban governance, yet existing solutions depend on tightly coupled vision-language pipelines, resulting in heavy computation, semantic ambiguity, and limited deployability on edge devices. In practical surveillance settings, this task should be treated not only as a recognition problem but also as a system-level modeling and decision problem. This article presents DecoVAD, a branch-specialized multimodal surveillance framework that assigns low-cost visual screening and explicit semantic reasoning to different but coordinated processing streams. The implicit branch encodes complementary scene context and action dynamics, followed by a distance-based temporal aggregator that substitutes quadratic dot-product attention with linear-complexity kernels. The explicit branch invokes YOLO-World to detect objects, actions, and attributes, and it converts these primitives into transactional data; a parallel tree-based miner uncovers high-confidence association rules that are aligned with CLIP embeddings to drive fine-grained localization. The branches are coordinated through shared visual embeddings and a learnable late-fusion gate. This architecture markedly reduces network depth, FLOPs, and memory footprint while mitigating prompt-induced bias toward long-tail behaviors. Experiments on XD-Violence and UCF-Crime show that DecoVAD matches or surpasses state-of-the-art performance while boosting inference speed and resource efficiency by up to 3×. Ablation studies confirm that rule-driven semantics enhance robustness in complex social scenes, and branchwise optimization facilitates rapid deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. © 2013 IEEE.},
