	abstract = {The management of diabetes increasingly relies on networked insulin pumps paired with continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) to stabilize blood sugar levels; though these systems face cyber risks including interception attacks and unauthorized dose adjustments. Encryption methods applied after data collection fail to verify the clinical validity of insulin instructions if the pump's internal system is breached. This study presents a TinyML-driven anomaly detection approach embedded within the pump's microcontroller to evaluate insulin-glucose dynamics in real time. Our method prevents harmful doses by spotting irregularities locally before network transfer, rather than just documenting them. This is complemented by a blockchain system for secure, tamper-proof storage and decentralized access to confirmed insulin events. Early feasibility studies, based on IoT security guidelines and basic blockchain tests, show that streamlined machine learning models can operate within the pump's limited capacity while maintaining effective detection performance. A full hardware prototype is yet to be developed, but this design offers robust defense against malicious insulin doses and supports wider use of on-device detection in medical IoT devices. Future efforts will focus on adjusting model thresholds for personal metabolic differences and validating performance through clinical trials.  © 2025 IEEE.},
